1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:01,748
August 21, 1911.

2
00:00:01,748 --> 00:00:03,216
The Louvre shut.

3
00:00:03,216 --> 00:00:08,067
By morning, the Mona Lisa was gone—and no one knew when.

4
00:00:08,357 --> 00:00:13,316
The blank wall raised no alarm: paintings came down for photos and cleaning.

5
00:00:13,316 --> 00:00:15,375
So the absence looked routine.

6
00:00:15,735 --> 00:00:19,842
Then the clue: Peruggia had worked inside, fitting protective glass.

7
00:00:19,842 --> 00:00:22,800
Investigators questioned him, but found no link.

8
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:24,567
The trail went cold.

9
00:00:24,857 --> 00:00:30,117
Two years later, he wrote Florence dealer Alfredo Geri, offering the painting.

10
00:00:30,117 --> 00:00:32,896
Geri called Uffizi director Giovanni Poggi.

11
00:00:32,896 --> 00:00:35,247
They checked it—and alerted police.

12
00:00:35,537 --> 00:00:39,255
Your turn: if this was patriotism, why offer it for sale?

13
00:00:39,255 --> 00:00:42,346
Peruggia said he wanted to return it to Italy.

14
00:00:42,346 --> 00:00:44,475
The offer left motive in doubt.

15
00:00:44,765 --> 00:00:49,820
In 1914, he was convicted and sentenced to one year and fifteen days.

16
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:52,680
The Mona Lisa returned to the Louvre.

17
00:00:52,970 --> 00:00:58,607
Final twist: the theft helped make a famous painting a global icon.

18
00:00:58,607 --> 00:00:59,180
Motive?

19
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:03,062
Peruggia claimed patriotism; the sale suggested another possibility.

20
00:01:03,062 --> 00:01:06,495
What do you think—belief, profit, or both?

